{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "18",
    "case_summary": "A 34-year-old woman reported experiencing intermittent lower abdominal pain and abdominal distension for five months. She did not have any gastrointestinal bleeding, nausea, vomiting, or changes in bowel habits. There was no history of seizures, cognitive issues, behavioral problems, or skin abnormalities. She had a caesarean section seven months prior. Physical examination revealed a midline reducible incisional hernia with minimal tenderness in the right iliac fossa. A computed tomography scan showed an unexplained density in the right iliac fossa, potentially related to the caecum or pericaecal area. An infraumbilical incisional hernia containing bowel loops was also observed. Colonoscopy revealed erythema at the ileo-caecal junction, but biopsies from this area were normal. Magnetic resonance imaging confirmed a mass in the right iliac fossa, separate from and lying inferior to the caecum. Both ovaries appeared normal. During surgery, a retroperitoneal mass was found and excised, and the hernia was repaired. The mass was well-circumscribed with a thin fibrous capsule and had a soft consistency. The cut surface displayed lobules of fatty tissue interspersed with bands and nodules of firm white tissue with a whorled appearance.\n\nMicroscopic examination showed a lesion composed of lobules of mature fatty tissue harbouring multiple variable sized nodules of smooth muscle fibres. The smooth muscle fibres were arranged in broad interlacing fascicles. No cytological atypia was seen and only one mitotic figure was identified in the multiple sections examined and no necrosis. No proliferation of medium-sized arteries with thick muscular walls was observed. The spindle cells were positive for smooth muscle actin and desmin and negative for CD34 and CD117. The staining for HMB-45 was not performed, as blocks were not available.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
